sources:
    {"title":"Lifecycle Greenhouse Gas Emissions","url":"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar5/wg3/","author":"IPCC","type":"research"}
    {"title":"Nuclear Energy and the Environment","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/nuclear-power-and-the-environment","author":"IAEA","type":"official"}
    {"title":"Land Use of Energy Sources","url":"https://www.nei.org/advantages/land-use","author":"Nuclear Energy Institute","type":"official"}

statement:
    Nuclear power plants pollute the environment and are not a clean energy source

reality:
    Nuclear energy produces zero carbon emissions during operation and has one of the smallest environmental footprints

explanation:
    Nuclear power is one of the cleanest energy sources available:

    **Zero Emissions During Operation:**
    - No CO2, methane, or other greenhouse gases
    - No air pollutants like SO2 or NOx
    - Only water vapor from cooling towers

    **Minimal Land Use:**
    - A nuclear plant on 1 square mile can power a city
    - Solar needs 75x more land for same power
    - Wind needs 360x more land

    **Lifecycle Emissions:**
    - 12g CO2/kWh (including construction)
    - Comparable to wind (11g) and solar (48g)
    - Far better than natural gas (490g) or coal (820g)

    **Waste Management:**
    - All waste is contained and monitored
    - Doesn't pollute air, water, or soil
    - Volume is tiny compared to other energy sources
